{
  "figure": "fig_ex07_conduction_delay",
  "caption": "Conduction delay does NOT diverge at the block threshold, contrary to the saddle-node expectation. Crossing the 0.1 cm gap takes 27.4 ms in a healthy strand and rises to 43.4 ms near threshold, but over the final three decades of approach the excess delay changes by only 15.5 %, saturating at 16.0 ms; a power-law fit returns -0.072 with R^2 = 0.59, not the predicted -0.5. Panel (d) gives the reason: a stalled front cannot outlast its own upstream source, which repolarises on the recovery timescale, so the ceiling falls as the recovery rate rises -- measured exponent -1.46 against the -1 a pure 1/eps cap would give, the excess steepness coming from eps also shifting rho_crit itself. The transition to block is therefore discontinuous in delay: the wave crosses within roughly the recovery time, or not at all.",
